Treasury bills are short-term sovereign claims; government and corporate bonds differ in credit, currency, and maturity risk.|A corporate bond adds issuer default exposure to rate risk.|A government bond is risk-free in every currency and horizon?|No|Yes|Only if widely traded
Municipal and agency claims differ by issuer; covered bonds add designated collateral; inflation-linked cash adjusts under specified terms.|Inflation-linked principal can rise with the contractual index.|Every inflation-linked bond hedges each investor's exact living costs?|No|Yes|Only at maturity
Coupons may be fixed, floating, zero, or step-based; day-count and frequency affect payments.|₹100 face with 8% annual coupon pays ₹8 yearly if specified.|An 8% coupon necessarily means 8% market yield?|No|Yes|Only above par
Clean price excludes accrued interest; dirty price includes it and generally determines cash settlement.|Clean ₹98 + accrued ₹2 gives dirty ₹100.|Settlement price here is…?|₹100|₹98|₹96
Bond price is the present value of promised cash under appropriate yield assumptions; default risk needs explicit treatment.|A one-year ₹110 payment discounted at 10% prices at ₹100.|Higher required yield normally makes a fixed-payment bond price…?|Lower|Higher|Unchanged
Current yield uses coupon/price; YTM solves promised-flow discounting; call and worst yields consider redemption terms.|₹8 coupon on ₹80 price gives current yield 10%.|Current yield includes the full capital gain to redemption?|No|Yes|Only for discounts
Spot rates discount individual maturities; forwards describe implied future periods; par yields price coupon bonds at par.|A two-year cash flow needs its two-year discount factor.|One quoted coupon is automatically the complete spot curve?|No|Yes|Only for governments
Yield curves relate yields to maturity; expectations, term premiums, and market segmentation help explain shape.|An inverted curve combines pricing expectations and premiums.|Inversion guarantees a recession on a fixed date?|No|Yes|Only in developed markets
Bootstrapping extracts discount factors sequentially from instrument prices and cash flows.|A known one-year factor helps solve a two-year coupon bond's remaining factor.|Bootstrapping requires consistent…?|Prices, conventions, and instruments|Only coupon labels|Only issuer logos
Macaulay duration measures PV-weighted timing; modified duration approximates yield sensitivity; effective duration models option-dependent cash changes.|Modified duration 5 implies about −5% price change for a 1 percentage-point yield rise.|The approximation is exact for all yield changes?|No|Yes|Only for bonds
Convexity improves nonlinear sensitivity estimates; key-rate durations isolate curve points.|A parallel shift can hide offsetting short- and long-rate exposures.|Which reveals those curve exposures?|Key-rate duration|Coupon alone|Face value alone
Credit spreads reflect issuer and market compensation beyond benchmarks; spread duration measures price sensitivity to spread changes.|Spread duration 4 and +1 percentage-point spread imply about −4% price change.|Spread widening usually helps an existing long bond price?|No|Yes|Only for high yield
Floating-rate coupons reset to a benchmark plus contractual spread; credit and reset risks remain.|Benchmark rises from 4% to 5% with 2% spread: next coupon rate becomes 7%.|Floating rate removes all bond risk?|No|Yes|Only with frequent resets
Calls favour issuers; puts favour holders; convertibles add equity conversion rights under specified terms.|An issuer may call a high-coupon bond when rates fall.|The call can limit investors'…?|Price upside|Principal accounting|Coupon history
Default changes contractual repayment; recovery depends on priority, collateral, and proceedings; covenants constrain behaviour.|A ₹100 claim recovers ₹40: simplified loss ₹60.|Collateral guarantees full recovery?|No|Yes|Only for property
Ladders stagger maturities; immunisation aligns assets and liabilities using cash-flow and sensitivity conditions.|A liability due in five years requires matching more than headline yield.|Immunisation is maintenance-free under every rate movement?|No|Yes|Only with government bonds
Attribution separates carry, rate, curve, spread, and selection contributions; relative value compares adjusted opportunities.|A portfolio gains from falling benchmark rates despite widening credit spreads.|Overall gain proves strong credit selection?|No|Yes|Only with positive coupons
